{
  "answer": "The first superuser is created by an idempotent `init_db` step that runs after database migrations, at container startup or when `prestart.sh` is run.\n\n**When it runs**\n- `backend/scripts/prestart.sh:7` runs `alembic upgrade head` to apply migrations.\n- `backend/scripts/prestart.sh:10` then runs `python app/initial_data.py`.\n- `backend/README.md:22` and `:45` show this script being run manually or through Docker Compose.\n- `deployment.md:74` says the prestart script applies migrations and creates the first superuser.\n\n**How it's created**\n- `backend/app/initial_data.py:11-13` opens a session and calls `init_db`.\n- `backend/app/core/db.py:24-26` looks up a user whose email equals `settings.FIRST_SUPERUSER`.\n- `backend/app/core/db.py:27-33` creates that user only if none exists. It uses `FIRST_SUPERUSER` and `FIRST_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD` with `is_superuser=True`, then calls `crud.create_user`.\n- Both values come from settings, declared at `backend/app/core/config.py:65-66` and read from the environment.\n\n**Behavior**\n- The lookup-before-insert makes repeated runs safe. Changing the password in `.env` does not update an existing superuser.\n- The tests use the same `init_db` path in `backend/tests/conftest.py:18`.",
